# ssh-proxy — SOCKS proxy over SSH

## Start a background SOCKS5 proxy through a jump host

```sh
ssh -D 8080 -f -C -N david@jumphost
```

## Point a browser at the proxy

```sh
google-chrome --proxy-server="socks5://localhost:8080" &
```

## Reach an internal homelab service through the tunnel

```sh
# Proxy is up on :8080 — route curl through it
curl --socks5-hostname localhost:8080 http://homelab-internal:8123
```

## Tear the tunnel down

```sh
pkill -f "ssh -D 8080"
```

## Flag reference

| Flag | What it does                          |
| ---- | ------------------------------------- |
| -D   | dynamic (SOCKS) port forward          |
| -f   | drop to background before command run |
| -C   | compress the traffic                  |
| -N   | do not run a remote command           |

> Use `socks5://` (proxy-side DNS) rather than `socks4` so lookups happen on the
> remote end and don't leak, and stay reachable inside the remote network.
